<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.18.7: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01200</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.18.7: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bc050223-a311-4f18-9577-6d3511fc2ac2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="99402f9a-a336-4a0e-a2d3-09edacc50f47" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of each specified term of the arithmetic sequences below. Hint: Enter your answers as an integer only. Do not include commas or periods.</para>
        <para>Find the \(21^{st}\) term of a sequence where the first term is \(3\) and the common difference is \(8\).
          </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 163</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 and 4.18.4. </para><para>The correct answer is \(163\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="ca8da07c-a1b2-47c5-8fc9-4f00d8795c60" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the \(30^{th}\) term of a sequence where the first term is \(–14\) and the common difference is \(5\).
          </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(131\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 and 4.18.4. </para><para>The correct answer is \(131\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="59a632cd-0c67-4b5d-abab-0c35a39ba7e3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the \(16^{th}\) term of a sequence where the first term is \(11\) and the common difference is \(–6\).
          </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! –79</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 and 4.18.4. </para><para>The correct answer is \(–79\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="39bd974f-5453-4265-8b24-c1864b65633f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of each specified term of the geometric sequences below. Hint: Enter your answers as integers only. Do not include commas or periods.</para>
        <para>Find the \(11^{th}\) term given the first term is \(8\) and the common ratio is \(3\).
          </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(472392\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(472392\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="1ebdbd24-382d-4498-a97e-36145e5f04d2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the \(10^{th}\) term given the first term is \(–6\) and the common ratio is \(–2\).
          </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 3072</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(3072\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="baf6f255-ad47-4bf3-8bcf-4ed32dbc4b92" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=-6, f_n=f_{n-1}-2\), for \(n≥2\). Which of the following gives the \(n^{th}\) term for \(n≥0\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a_{n}=-2+(n-1)(-6) \)</para><para>\(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \)</para><para>\(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)+(-2)\)</para><para>\(a_{n}=-6+(n)(-2)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.5. </para><para>The correct answer is \(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="38258be5-0b3a-4ef7-ab27-c604a6fdace1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=-20, f_n=f_{(n-1)}+5\) for \(n≥2\). Which of the following is the \(n^{th}\) term?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)+5\)</para><para>\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(-2)\)</para><para>\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\)</para><para>\(a_{n}=5+(n-1)(-20)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="6fffcf18-e6ac-4983-9fe7-1963ef7b5157" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=3\), \(f_n=2 \cdot f_{n-1}\), \(n\geq2\). Which of the following gives the \(n^{th}\) term for \(n\geq0\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f_n=3 \cdot 2^{n-1}\)</para><para>\(f_n=3^n\)</para><para>\(f_n=3+2n\)</para><para>\(f_n=3+2^n\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(f_n=3 \cdot 2^{n-1}\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(f_n=3 \cdot 2^{n-1}\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="ce6668d2-9242-4870-bca6-77ffc2b01910" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A sequence of numbers is given by \(a_2=1\), \(a_3=5\), \(a_4=9\), \(a_5=13\), \(a_6=17\). Which expression can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(4n-3\)</para><para>\(4n-7\)</para><para>\(7n-4\)</para><para>\(3n-4\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(4n-7\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(4n-7\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="4ff598c9-dc47-43e1-95a4-4660909eca7c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A sequence of numbers is given by \(\frac{1}{27}\), \(\frac19\), \(\frac13\), 1, …. What is the equation that can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}-\frac{1}{3}(n-1)\)</para><para>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(\frac{1}{3})^{n-1}\)</para><para>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\)</para><para>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}\div3(n-1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="534149b6-1201-4140-aeed-e7efc99df673" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A sequence is given by the set of numbers 5, 1, -3, -7 … What is the equation that can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a_n = 5 + (n-1)(-4)\)</para><para>\(a_n = 5(-4)^{n-1}\)</para><para>\(a_n = 5(4)^{n-1}\)</para><para>\(a_n = 5 - (n-1)(-4)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a_n = 5 + (n-1)(-4)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(a_n = 5 + (n-1)(-4)\). </para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Do not edit this div tag.  It must surround the interaction block-->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="a213731c-aa53-490d-a01a-b1b95c11851b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A sequence consists of the following terms: 1280, 320, 80, … What is the explicit formula for this sequence? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a_n = 1280 - 960(n-1)\)</para><para>\(a_n = 1280 (4)^{n-1}\)</para><para>\(a_n = 1280 (\frac 14)^{n-1}\)</para><para>\(a_n = 1280 \div 4(n-1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a_n = 1280 (\frac 14)^{n-1}\) </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(a_n = 1280 (\frac 14)^{n-1}\) . </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="4756240a-7f43-40ce-930b-8c33a8a7c052" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>An arithmetic sequence consists of the terms 1, 6, 11, 16 …. If you were asked to find the \(100^{th}\) term, what is the general formula you would use to find that value efficiently? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\)</para><para>\(a_n = 1 (a_{n-1})^5\)</para><para>\(a_n = 5+ a_{n-1}\)</para><para>\(a_n = 1 + 5(a_{n-1})\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\). </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
